===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}
According to the {{w|Shuowen Jiezi}}, a {{liushu|p|nocap=yes}} of an animal, with {{lang|zh|吅}} for its ears and {{lang|zh|田}} for its head. The feet, originally depicted as {{lang|zh|厹}} in the variant {{lang|zh|𠾧}}, simplified as {{lang|zh|口}}.<ref>"（嘼）㹌也。象耳、頭、足厹地之形。古文嘼，下从厹。凡嘼之屬皆从嘼。許救切"</ref>

The Shuowen explanation of the character conflicts with several scholars. {{zh-ref|Huang Dekuan}} considers the character to be a compound of {{lang|zh|[[單]]}} with {{lang|zh|口}} added as a distinguishing mark,<ref>黃德寬，2007《古文字譜系疏證》，北京：商務印書館。 p. 613-614</ref> making it a {{liushu|psc|nocap=yes}}. This aligns with {{zh-ref|Ji Xusheng}}'s criticism of the pronunciation given in the Shuowen ({{w|fanqie}}  {{lang|zh|許救切}}). He argues that there is no evidence supporting it, while there's proof of it instead being pronounced the same as {{och-l|單}}. According to him, however, {{lang|zh|嘼}} should be a {{liushu|2|nocap=yes}} of {{lang|zh|[[獸]]}}, with the {{lang|zh|犬}} component omitted. <ref>季旭昇，2004《說文新證》，台北：藝文印書館印行，2014年9月第二版。p. 956</ref>

